<h2>India's largest chilli and cumin market</h2>
<p>China isn't a market most buyers associate with Indian spice exports, but the trade data is unambiguous: China is India's single largest destination for dried red chilli by a wide margin, and the largest for cumin as well. Fish meal and sesame seeds add further volume.</p>

<h2>GACC registration is the gate you have to clear first</h2>
<p>China's Decree 248 requires facility-level registration with the General Administration of Customs of China (GACC) before many food products can be imported — this applies to the producing/processing facility, not just the trading relationship. If you're sourcing a product from us for the first time into China, confirm registration status early; it's a process that takes real lead time and can't be resolved at the point of shipment.</p>

<h2>China sets its own standards — don't assume EU or US compliance transfers</h2>
<p>This is worth stating plainly because it trips up buyers moving between markets. A shipment tested and cleared to EU MRLs is not automatically compliant with Chinese standards, which are set independently. Tell us China is your destination from the start of the conversation, and we'll test and document against the applicable Chinese requirements rather than assuming another market's clearance carries over.</p>

<h2>What to expect on transit and payment</h2>
<p>Transit runs 15–22 days to major Chinese ports — mid-range compared with our other lanes. Payment practices among established Chinese trading houses are generally efficient, with both LC and TT common depending on the relationship.</p>

